      "writtenOn": "2026-07-14T17:26:36Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "**Issue:**\nIn `fp_command_context()`, the patch correctly prevents malicious mid-session user swaps by returning `EC_RES_ACCESS_DENIED` if a user ID is already set (`FP_CONTEXT_USER_ID_SET`). However, this logic also blocks **legitimate, idempotent retries** from the Application Processor (AP). If the AP\u0027s IPC mechanism times out and it re-transmits the `GET_RESULT` command with the *same* user ID, the EC will deny access, potentially breaking the AP\u0027s state machine.\n\n**Suggestion:**\nAllow idempotent calls by checking if the requested user ID matches the currently active user ID before denying access.\n\nAdditionally, since `global_context.user_id` is a `std::array` and the codebase is utilizing C++20, we can replace the C-style memory operations (`memcpy`) with safer, type-aware `std::ranges` algorithms for both the comparison and the subsequent assignment.\n\nHere is how you can update the logic:\n\n```cpp\n\t\tif (fingerprint_auth_enabled() \u0026\u0026\n\t\t    (global_context.fp_encryption_status \u0026\n\t\t     FP_CONTEXT_USER_ID_SET)) {\n\t\t\t/* Allow idempotent retries from the AP */\n\t\t\tif (std::ranges::equal(global_context.user_id, p-\u003euserid)) {\n\t\t\t\treturn EC_RES_SUCCESS;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\treturn EC_RES_ACCESS_DENIED;\n\t\t}\n\n```",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 377,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 381,
        "endChar": 3
      },
      "revId": "dd9015496dc8a3018a11dd3bbbabb2126cc9db4e",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "fd5fa87a_2f593320",
        "filename": "common/fpsensor/fpsensor_state.cc",
